L = {}
L["peter-escape"] = """By this time, the church in Jerusalem was growing, and trouble was growing with it. King Herod Agrippa, a grandson of the Herod who tried to kill baby Jesus, wanted to win favor with the Jewish leaders. He arrested some members of the church and had James, the brother of John, killed with a sword. James was one of the first three disciples, and one of Jesus' closest friends. When Herod saw that this pleased the people, he arrested Peter too. This happened during the Festival of Unleavened Bread, around Passover. Herod planned to put Peter on trial after the festival.

Peter was put in prison and guarded by four squads of four soldiers each. That means sixteen men, watching him in shifts. The night before the trial, Peter was sleeping between two soldiers, bound with two chains, and there were guards at the door. There was no way out. Herod made sure of it.

But the Bible says, the church was earnestly praying to God for him. That is the most important sentence in the story. The believers did not have a plan, an army, or any power. They had prayer.

Suddenly an angel of the Lord appeared, and a light shone in the cell. He struck Peter on the side and woke him up. Quick, get up! he said, and the chains fell off Peter's wrists. The angel told him, put on your clothes and sandals. Peter did. Wrap your cloak around you and follow me. Peter followed him out of the prison, but he had no idea that what the angel was doing was really happening. He thought he was seeing a vision.

They passed the first and second guards and came to the iron gate leading to the city. It opened for them by itself, and they went through it. When they had walked the length of one street, suddenly the angel left him. Then Peter came to himself and said, now I know without a doubt that the Lord sent his angel and rescued me from Herod's clutches and from everything the Jewish people were anticipating.

Peter went to the house of Mary, the mother of John Mark, where many people had gathered and were praying. He knocked at the outer entrance, and a servant named Rhoda came to answer the door. When she recognized Peter's voice, she was so overjoyed she forgot to open it. Instead she ran back and exclaimed, Peter is at the door! You're out of your mind, they told her. When she kept insisting, they said, it must be his angel. But Peter kept on knocking, and when they opened the door and saw him, they were astonished.

It is a funny moment, and an honest one. These people had been praying all night for Peter's release, and when God answered, they could hardly believe it. Peter motioned for them to be quiet and described how the Lord had brought him out of prison. Tell James and the other brothers and sisters about this, he said. Then he left for another place.

In the morning, there was no small commotion among the soldiers about what had become of Peter. Herod had a thorough search made, and when he did not find him, he cross-examined the guards and ordered that they be executed. Soon after, Herod gave a speech, and the people shouted, this is the voice of a god, not of a man. Immediately, because Herod did not give praise to God, an angel of the Lord struck him down, and he was eaten by worms and died. But the word of God continued to spread and flourish.

Why does this matter? Two apostles were arrested by the same king. One, James, was put to death. The other, Peter, was set free. The Bible does not explain why. It shows us that God's ways are not always the same, and that both stories are part of his plan. We don't get to know why one is rescued and another is not. But both are in his hands.

It also shows the power of the church praying together. They didn't have influence or weapons. They had one another and God. The same Peter who denied Jesus and ran away now slept peacefully in chains, the night before a trial that could have ended in his death.

What happened after? Peter left Jerusalem, and we see less of him in the book of Acts. Tradition says that he eventually went to Rome. The Bible doesn't say exactly, but tradition has said that he was put to death there, as Jesus had said he would be. The church kept growing.

What does this mean for us today? When something looks hopeless, pray. Not as a last resort, but as a first response. The believers in this story didn't know the outcome. They prayed anyway, through the night, while the chains were still on.

And when God answers, don't be surprised if you find it hard to believe, like Rhoda's friends. That is a very human thing. God is gracious to people who ask, and a little slow to believe.

That is why this story is so important. It shows that the power of earthly kings is limited. Herod had soldiers, chains, and iron gates, and none of it held. The church's greatest strength was a prayer meeting in a house."""

L["paul-prison"] = """Paul and his friend Silas had come to Philippi, a Roman city in Macedonia, in what is now Greece. They had come because of a vision. Paul saw a man from Macedonia begging him, come over to Macedonia and help us. On the Sabbath, they went to a place of prayer by the river, and spoke to some women who had gathered there. One of them, a businesswoman named Lydia, who sold purple cloth, listened. The Lord opened her heart, and she and her household were baptized. Philippi had its first church.

Soon after, they met a slave girl who had a spirit by which she predicted the future. She earned a great deal of money for her owners by fortune-telling. She followed Paul and the rest of them, shouting, these men are servants of the Most High God, who are telling you the way to be saved. She did this for many days. Finally Paul became so troubled that he turned around and said to the spirit, in the name of Jesus Christ I command you to come out of her! At that moment it left her.

When her owners realized that their hope of making money was gone, they seized Paul and Silas and dragged them into the marketplace to face the authorities. These men are Jews, and are throwing our city into an uproar by advocating customs unlawful for us Romans to adopt. A crowd joined in the attack. The magistrates ordered them to be stripped and beaten with rods. After they had been severely flogged, they were thrown into prison, and the jailer was commanded to guard them carefully. Receiving such orders, he put them in the inner cell and fastened their feet in the stocks.

Think about the picture. Paul and Silas had done nothing wrong. They had brought healing to a girl, and they had been beaten, bleeding, and chained in the darkest cell in the city. And what did they do? About midnight Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns to God, and the other prisoners were listening to them.

Suddenly there was such a violent earthquake that the foundations of the prison were shaken. At once all the prison doors flew open, and everyone's chains came loose. The jailer woke up, and when he saw the prison doors open, he drew his sword and was about to kill himself because he thought the prisoners had escaped. In the Roman world, a jailer who lost his prisoners could be executed. But Paul shouted, don't harm yourself! We are all here!

The jailer called for lights, rushed in, and fell trembling before Paul and Silas. He then brought them out and asked, sirs, what must I do to be saved? They replied, believe in the Lord Jesus, and you will be saved, you and your household. Then they spoke the word of the Lord to him and to all the others in his house. At that hour of the night, the jailer took them and washed their wounds. Then immediately he and all his household were baptized. The jailer brought them into his house and set a meal before them. He was filled with joy because he had come to believe in God, he and his whole household.

In the morning the magistrates sent word to release them. But Paul said, they beat us publicly without a trial, even though we are Roman citizens, and threw us into prison. And now do they want to get rid of us quietly? No! Let them come themselves and escort us out. When the officials heard that Paul and Silas were Roman citizens, they were alarmed. They came to appease them and escorted them out of the prison, requesting them to leave the city.

Why does this matter? Paul and Silas chose to sing in the dark. They didn't know there would be an earthquake. They sang before the answer came. Their joy didn't depend on the circumstances. It is one of the best pictures of faith in the New Testament. Later Paul would write to the church at Philippi, from another prison, rejoice in the Lord always. I will say it again, rejoice!

It also shows how God uses hard places. The jailer and his family would never have heard the gospel if Paul had not been put in that cell. A beating, chains, and a night in the stocks led to a whole family coming to Jesus.

What happened after? The church in Philippi grew and stayed close to Paul. They were the one church that sent him money again and again. Paul's letter to the Philippians is a letter of thanks and joy, written to the church that began with a businesswoman, a freed slave girl, and a jailer.

What does this mean for us today? When life puts you in the inner cell, with no way out, you still have a choice about what to do at midnight. Praise doesn't change the walls. But it changes you, and the people listening. The other prisoners heard them.

It also gives the simplest answer to the most important question in the Bible. What must I do to be saved? Believe in the Lord Jesus. It is no more complicated than that, and the jailer was baptized that very night.

That is why this story is so important. It shows that God is at work in our worst nights, and that the gospel is for anyone, from a rich businesswoman to a rough jailer. And it shows that the song of a believer can make a prison tremble."""

L["thessalonica"] = """After Philippi, Paul and Silas traveled about a hundred miles west along a great Roman road, the Via Egnatia, and came to Thessalonica. It was the capital of Macedonia, a busy port city. There was a Jewish synagogue there, and as was his custom, Paul went in. On three Sabbath days he reasoned with them from the Scriptures, explaining and proving that the Messiah had to suffer and rise from the dead. This Jesus I am proclaiming to you is the Messiah, he said.

Some of the Jews were persuaded and joined Paul and Silas, as did a large number of God-fearing Greeks and not a few prominent women. These were people who were interested in the God of Israel but were not Jewish themselves. For them, this message of grace was good news.

But other Jews were jealous. They rounded up some bad characters from the marketplace, formed a mob, and started a riot in the city. They rushed to Jason's house, where Paul and Silas had been staying, in search of them. When they didn't find them, they dragged Jason and some other believers before the city officials, shouting, these men who have caused trouble all over the world have now come here! Jason has welcomed them into his house. They are all defying Caesar's decrees, saying that there is another king, one called Jesus.

The charge was partly true. The Christians did say that Jesus was king. But they were not rebelling against Rome. Their loyalty to Jesus was just deeper than any other loyalty. Jason had to post bond, and the city officials let them go. That night, the believers sent Paul and Silas away to Berea.

Notice the line, men who have caused trouble all over the world. Other translations say, these men who have turned the world upside down. A few men, with no weapons and no army, had so changed the lives of the people they met that their opponents said the world was being turned upside down.

In Berea, the Jews were of more noble character than those in Thessalonica, for they received the message with great eagerness and examined the Scriptures every day to see if what Paul said was true. Many of them believed. But when some Jews from Thessalonica heard that Paul was preaching in Berea, they went there to stir up the crowds. So Paul had to move on to Athens.

Paul was worried about the new believers he had left behind. He wrote to them from Corinth, and the letter, First Thessalonians, is probably the oldest book in the New Testament. In it he said, we always thank God for all of you. We remember before our God and Father your work produced by faith, your labor prompted by love, and your endurance inspired by hope in our Lord Jesus Christ. You became imitators of us and of the Lord, for you welcomed the message in the midst of severe suffering with the joy given by the Holy Spirit.

He also gave them hope about the ones who had died. Brothers and sisters, we do not want you to be uninformed about those who sleep in death, so that you do not grieve like the rest of mankind, who have no hope. For we believe that Jesus died and rose again, and so we believe that God will bring with Jesus those who have fallen asleep in him.

Why does this matter? Thessalonica shows what the first church was like. These were new believers, in a hostile city, with a few weeks of teaching. And they became a model church. The faith they had was real, and they were willing to suffer for it. It also shows that the gospel goes forward in the face of opposition, and often because of it.

The Bereans are a model too. They did not just accept what Paul said. They checked it against the Bible every day. That is exactly how we should hear any teacher, with eagerness, and with the Scriptures open.

What happened after? Paul went on to Athens and Corinth, and the church at Thessalonica kept growing. Paul wrote them a second letter. They remained one of the churches he was most proud of. Today, the city is still there, called Thessaloniki, one of the largest cities in Greece.

What does this mean for us today? Do you want to turn the world upside down? Most of the people who did it in the first century were ordinary. They worked at jobs, opened their homes, and told people about Jesus. A few of them, like Jason, took risks to host a meeting.

It also means we have hope in grief. Paul told them not to grieve like people who have no hope. When someone we love who belonged to Jesus dies, we will see them again. That promise is not a small thing.

That is why this story is so important. It shows the gospel arriving in a new city, and how the first believers lived. They were joyful, brave, and clear about their hope. And it reminds us that when the Word is believed, the world is never quite the same."""

L["patmos"] = """John was the last of the twelve apostles still alive. He had been the young disciple Jesus loved, who leaned against him at the Last Supper and stood at the foot of the cross. Jesus had trusted him to look after his mother. Now he was an old man, probably in his nineties, and he had spent decades leading the churches around Ephesus, in what is now Turkey. Peter, James, and Paul were all long gone. Nearly all of the other apostles had been put to death for their faith.

Now there was a new wave of persecution. The Roman emperor Domitian demanded that people call him lord and god, and Christians refused. They would not say anyone but Jesus was Lord. Because of this, John was arrested and sent into exile on Patmos, a small, rocky island in the Aegean Sea, about forty miles off the coast. It was a place the Romans sent prisoners. He was there because of the word of God and the testimony of Jesus.

Think about what this must have been like. John was old, alone, and cut off from the churches he had cared for. He had lost his friends and his freedom. It could have been the worst time of his life. But it was on this island that John received one of the most amazing experiences in the Bible.

On the Lord's Day, which was Sunday, John was in the Spirit. He heard behind him a loud voice like a trumpet, which said, write on a scroll what you see and send it to the seven churches. He turned around to see the voice that was speaking to him. And when he turned, he saw seven golden lampstands, and among the lampstands was someone like a son of man, dressed in a robe reaching down to his feet. His head and hair were white like wool, as white as snow, and his eyes were like blazing fire. His feet were like bronze glowing in a furnace, and his voice was like the sound of rushing waters. In his right hand he held seven stars, and out of his mouth came a sharp, double-edged sword. His face was like the sun shining in all its brilliance.

It was Jesus, but not the Jesus John remembered from Galilee. This was the risen and glorified Lord. John had known him as a friend, and had rested his head on his chest. Now when he saw him, he fell at his feet as though dead.

Then Jesus placed his right hand on John and said, do not be afraid. I am the First and the Last. I am the Living One. I was dead, and now look, I am alive forever and ever! And I hold the keys of death and Hades. These are the words that John needed most in exile. Whoever holds the keys of death is not afraid of Rome.

Jesus then told John to write down what he saw. First were letters to seven churches in Asia, with praise for what they were doing well and warnings about what they needed to fix. To the church at Ephesus, you have forsaken the love you had at first. To Smyrna, be faithful, even to the point of death, and I will give you life as your victor's crown. To Laodicea, here I am! I stand at the door and knock. If anyone hears my voice and opens the door, I will come in and eat with that person, and they with me.

Why does this matter? This is the beginning of the book of Revelation, the last book in the Bible. It was written to encourage persecuted Christians. They were being told that the emperor was lord, and Jesus told them, no, I am. The seven churches were small and weak, but they were in his hand, and he walked among them.

It also shows that God does not abandon his people in the worst places. John was banished to the end of the earth, and that was where Jesus appeared to him.

What happened after? John wrote down everything he saw, the visions of heaven, the final battle, and the new heaven and new earth. He sent it to the churches. Tradition says that after Domitian died, John was released and went back to Ephesus, where he died of old age. He was the only apostle who did not die as a martyr. The Bible doesn't say this exactly, but tradition has said it.

What does this mean for us today? If you are in your own Patmos, a place of exile, loneliness, or loss, you are not out of reach. Jesus found John on a rocky island, and he finds people in hospital rooms, empty houses, and hard seasons.

It also means that the one who holds the keys to death is not just a memory. He is alive. And his first words to a frightened old man are the words he says to us. Do not be afraid.

That is why this story is so important. It opens the last book of the Bible with a picture of Jesus as he is now, living, ruling, and walking among his people. No emperor is above him, and no island is beyond him."""

L["new-jerusalem"] = """The Bible ends with a vision of the future. John, exiled on Patmos, was shown the end of the story, and he wrote it down for all of us. After many visions of judgment and the defeat of evil, he saw something new. Then I saw a new heaven and a new earth, for the first heaven and the first earth had passed away, and there was no longer any sea.

The old world is gone. The world that was broken by sin, filled with pain, war, and death, has given way to something new. And then John saw the holy city, the new Jerusalem, coming down out of heaven from God, prepared as a bride beautifully dressed for her husband. This is the opposite of Babel. The people tried to build a tower up to heaven, and failed. Here, God brings the city down to us.

John heard a loud voice from the throne saying, look! God's dwelling place is now among the people, and he will dwell with them. They will be his people, and God himself will be with them and be their God. He will wipe every tear from their eyes. There will be no more death or mourning or crying or pain, for the old order of things has passed away.

Read that slowly. He will wipe every tear from their eyes. Not a general feeling of comfort, but a personal act. God himself, close enough to wipe away tears.

He who was seated on the throne said, I am making everything new! Then he said, write this down, for these words are trustworthy and true. He said, it is done. I am the Alpha and the Omega, the Beginning and the End. To the thirsty I will give water without cost from the spring of the water of life. Those who are victorious will inherit all this, and I will be their God and they will be my children.

An angel then showed John the city. It shone with the glory of God, and its brilliance was like that of a very precious jewel, like a jasper, clear as crystal. It had a great, high wall with twelve gates, with twelve angels at the gates, and on the gates were written the names of the twelve tribes of Israel. The wall had twelve foundations, and on them were the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb. The city was laid out like a square, as long as it was wide, and the streets were pure gold, like transparent glass. The gates of the city were never shut, because there is no night there.

I did not see a temple in the city, John wrote, because the Lord God Almighty and the Lamb are its temple. The city does not need the sun or the moon to shine on it, for the glory of God gives it light, and the Lamb is its lamp. The nations will walk by its light, and the kings of the earth will bring their splendor into it.

Then the angel showed him the river of the water of life, as clear as crystal, flowing from the throne of God and of the Lamb down the middle of the great street of the city. On each side of the river stood the tree of life, bearing twelve crops of fruit, yielding its fruit every month. The leaves of the tree are for the healing of the nations. No longer will there be any curse.

Do you see what happened? The garden of Eden is back. The tree of life that was guarded by angels at the beginning is now open to everyone. The curse that began in Genesis is gone. The story that began in a garden ends in a city that has a garden in it.

The throne of God and of the Lamb will be in the city, and his servants will serve him. They will see his face, and his name will be on their foreheads. There will be no more night. They will not need the light of a lamp or the light of the sun, for the Lord God will give them light. And they will reign for ever and ever.

Why does this matter? It is the end of the Bible, and it is the promise that the end is good. Everything God started in Genesis, he finishes. Evil does not win. Death does not have the last word. The relationship broken in Eden is fully restored, with God living among his people, face to face.

The book closes with an invitation. The Spirit and the bride say, come! And let the one who hears say, come! Let the one who is thirsty come, and let the one who wishes take the free gift of the water of life. And the last prayer in the Bible is, come, Lord Jesus.

What does this mean for us today? It means that what we're living now is not all there is. If you have lost someone, are in pain, or are weary of a broken world, this is your hope. One day God will wipe every tear. That is not wishful thinking. It is a promise from the one who sits on the throne, who says these words are trustworthy and true.

It also means that what we do now matters. The city is coming, and we are called to live as its citizens. We are to love, forgive, and serve now in a way that shows what's coming.

That is why this story is so important. It is the last page of the Bible, and it answers the question of how the story ends. It ends with God and his people together, forever, in a world made new. Whoever is thirsty can come."""
